WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.190
A content delivery network keeps copies of popular files close to the people who watch them.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.003
Think of a bakery chain.

00:00:08.453 --> 00:00:12.597
Baking everything in one central factory means long delivery trips.

00:00:13.047 --> 00:00:20.454
So each neighborhood shop keeps the most popular bread on its shelves, and only orders from the factory when it runs out.

00:00:20.904 --> 00:00:24.527
The shops are edge caches, and the factory is the origin.

00:00:25.877 --> 00:00:27.386
Here is a request path.

00:00:27.836 --> 00:00:30.936
A viewer asks the nearest edge for a video chunk.

00:00:31.386 --> 00:00:33.894
On a hit the edge answers immediately.

00:00:34.344 --> 00:00:39.858
On a miss it asks a regional shield cache, and only then the origin, then keeps a copy.

00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:43.577
Words to use in a design interview.

00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:49.449
Hit ratio, the share of requests served from cache, drives cost and latency.

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:53.347
Time to live and versioned file names decide freshness.

00:00:53.797 --> 00:00:59.997
Netflix pre-positions popular titles on its Open Connect appliances overnight, before people press play.

00:01:01.347 --> 00:01:07.918
Cache popular content at the edge, shield the origin, and plan freshness with TTLs and versioned names.
